<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikola Petkov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikola Petkov was a Bulgarian politician, leader of the Bulgarian Agrarian National Union and of the opposition to communist rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. A minister in the Fatherland Front government of 1944–45, he broke with the communists and led the opposition in the elections of 1946. He was arrested in 1947, convicted in a show trial and hanged in September 1947, despite protests from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikola-petkov.jpg" alt="Nikola Petkov" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikola_Petkov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikola Dimitrov Petkov was born on 8 July 1893, a son of the politician Dimitar Petkov; his brother Petko Petkov was shot dead by an unknown assassin in 1924. He graduated from the 1st Sofia Boys High School in 1910 and studied law and politics at the Sorbonne, returning to serve in a Guards regiment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913. He finished his studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1922 and worked at the Bulgarian legation there, but resigned after the coup of 9 June 1923 removed the agrarian government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandar-stamboliyski">Aleksandar Stamboliyski</a>, and stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a journalist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1929, he edited the newspapers Zemya and Zemedelsko zname and published a book on Stamboliyski. After the coup of 19 May 1934 he worked with the democratic parties, among them the Labours' Party, the legal organisation of the banned Bulgarian Communist Party. Elected to the 24th National Assembly, he lost his seat in December 1938 for his anti-fascist activity and was interned at Ivailovgrad. In 1941, after G. M. Dimitrov emigrated, he took over the leadership of his wing of the BZNS, was sent to the Gonda Voda camp, and helped to found the Fatherland Front, work he continued while interned at Svishtov from 1943.</p><p>From 9 September 1944 to 26 August 1945 he was minister without portfolio in the first Fatherland Front government. From January 1945 he led the anti-communist United opposition, and from 26 November 1946 he sat in the 6th Great National Assembly. Mark Ethridge, sent by the State Department in 1945 to report on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> to President Truman, called him the bravest man he had ever known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The communists treated his defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> as counter-revolutionary. His immunity was lifted on 5 June 1947 and he was arrested inside the parliament building, where colleagues who tried to stop it were beaten. At a staged trial with four co-defendants, in which the defence was denied legal representation and the right to present evidence, he was convicted of espionage and sentenced to death on 16 August. He was hanged on 23 September 1947, denied a Christian burial and buried in an unknown grave; a false confession printed afterwards was so plainly faked that it was soon dropped. He was rehabilitated on 15 January 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikola Petkov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Communist Party nationalised industry, collectivised agriculture and crushed opposition, executing the Agrarian leader <b>Nikola Petkov</b> in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-bulgaria">People's Republic of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.7,23.32&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Sofia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5114 646 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikola Petkov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5564 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5564 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.7,23.32&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
